{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot","id":1547856,"title":"Maybe we should split the Metaplot.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-01-20T12:31:19-08:00","id":1547856,"title":"Maybe we should split the Metaplot.","content":"The five main lines of the WoD don't belong together. At least not anymore. I once joked that V:tM, W:tA, and W:tO, were the Damned, the Doomed, and the Lost. But that's literal. Vampires are mainly &quot;Damned if they do and damned if they don't.&quot; Werewolves are bound by fate, their doom, and unable to change that, and Wraiths are very much lost souls. <br /> <br /> Mages and Changelings are profoundly different. They are striving to be the heroes of their own tales creating and rewriting their destinies as they go. <br /> <br /> The first three can fit together and so can the second, but all five lead to incoherence. Vampire, Werewolf, and Wraith, are like Macbeth, Hamlet, King Lear, and Othello, tragedies. Mage and Changeling combine As You Like It, Much Ado about Nothing, The Tempest, and Alls Well That Ends Well. With Changeling pulling more from the first two and Mage pulling more from the last two. <br /> <br /> I think it would be creatively good for there to be one metaplot for the tragic trio and another for the comic duo. <br /> <br /> Other game lines (Hunter, Demon, Mummy, and Kindred of the East) could be placed where they fit best. <br /> <br /> Note: All types of Supernaturals would be in each Metaplot. But the supernaturals that don't fit that particular Metaplot would be sidelined. <br /> <br /> Reactions? Refinements? Questions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-20T13:59:46-08:00","id":1547862,"title":"","content":"So reactions:<br /> <br /> 1) Is there even a point to caring any more? WoD5 is basically an entirely different thing now, and the pre-WoD5 continuity is no longer officially supported. There are no new WoD books coming out with the old takes on the games, so which games get along with which is ultimately down to table preference for people still playing the pre-WoD5 games.<br /> <br /> 2) The rationale here is so incredibly surface that I'm kinda shocked you're posting it given you're generally more in-depth takes on things. Calling Mage and Changeling Shakespearean comedies feel like a complete lack of understanding of either side of that statement. All of the core initial five WoD game lines are tragedies, and it's not hard to line them up to Shakespeare more directly: Vampire is Othello and Titus, where political maneuvering and plays for power bring out the most brutal and viscous aspects of human nature. Werewolf is Macbeth and Hamlet, where honored warriors are pressed to seek even greater station in life instead of pursuit of what they know is a more moral path to their own destruction. Wraith is Romeo and Juliet, and Antony and Cleopatra, stories of deep passions driving characters to both the greatest highs and lowest lows, but ultimately futile as it all comes to naught. Mage is Timon of Athens where a well meaning man of wealth is incautious with his power and only brings sorrow where he thought he planted joy, and Coriolanus where pride and shifting allegiances turn all against the self-righteous hero. Changeling is Troilus and Cressida and King Lear, where duty and folly mix to the verge of comedy before falling into pain and violence.<br /> <br /> 3) The idea that the ease of making a metaplot work is based on the tone of the individual games is misguided. A lot of the metaplot debates (to be polite) over the past 30 years have been around the metaphysical inconsistencies of the differing games. Vampire and Wraith are usually their own little islands that don't interact nicely with Werewolf, Mage, and Changeling who have more easily reconciled metaphysical outlooks. The tone of the games might be different, but putting the Garou and the Traditions in the same universe is not as difficult as trying to figure out how to make Wraiths relevant in a world with necromancers like the Giovani around.<br /> <br /> -------------<br /> <br /> So I guess, ultimately, what's the point? This is a very old debate, and one that's never really been well settled. Everyone has their own priorities, levels of tolerance for disparities between the games, and so on. The &quot;metaplot&quot; is dead (since there's not really any future metaplot to happen now to really consider it one), and the games' audiences aren't exactly thriving. The WoD fandom kinda needs to be one thing to endure, not five different things. The desire to get every game line to &quot;play together&quot; well in one more unified shared universe is what drives people to use the CofD and is a bit draw of Cursborne as well. The idiosyncratic oddities of the shared WoD universe are basically what keeps people coming back to it.<br /> <br /> Or to put it another way... how does it make things any better to try to &quot;settle&quot; the argument of which games work well together as some sort of objective thing to cluster folks around?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-11T21:52:57-08:00","id":1556501,"title":"","content":"In general, I just never liked the doomsday metaplots. I liked the ideas behind Y2K, and that behind the mundane events of IRL January 1, 2000 - the entire supernatural world went through a giant storm that kinda-sorta soft-reset things.<br /> <br /> Which... is kinda exactly what happens in the 20th Anniversary editions (?)<br /> <br /> Take the Prophecy of the Phoenix, combine it with the Avatar Storm... And then have an aftermath where there's room for everyone to breathe again. The setting stops being depressing, and instead gives the players the opportunity to rebuild Nodes and Caerns from the ashes - to be reborn, in a sense (like the Phoenix prophecy). It opens up and becomes more sandbox-like.<br /> <br /> The stories are now more slice-of-life, rags-to-riches, adventurous, and so-on.<br /> <br /> Here, there be a world worth residing in.<br /> <br /> I mean... the whole point of the comic in W20 is to show the reader &quot;Hey! We're still here! We're still alive! There's still HOPE!&quot;<br /> And that to me, is by far WAY more appealing than whatever outright nonsense Hunter: the Reckoning was supposed to be (outside of an Antagonist &quot;Sabbat but Hunters&quot; splat that bamboozles me that it's supposed to be a player splat instead of an oddly detailed Antagonist faction - on top of the fact that there are people who worship the ground of hot garbage that are the H:tR books), or just the load of disappointment that was the Time of Judgement on a general level.<br /> <br /> <u><b>TL : Dr</b></u><br /> Pretend the metaplot doesn't exist beyond &quot;an event in the past&quot; and just run your games as you see fit, with the overarching theme being the concept of a Phoenix and rebuilding. That's all I can really offer. Because what the pre-9/11 lore of oWoD offers is hot garbage.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, go ahead and read bits and pieces of it off of whatever wiki and form a picture in your mind what it could've been, in the case your games have an NPC that survived some aspect of it. More fun that way.<br /> <br /> Imagine a series of stories for Werewolf, where your players are rediscovering the Totems of the Tribes, and you're trying to rebuild the Garou nation. That'd be cool!<br /> Imagine a Mage Game where the players are able to picture the world as large shards of a broken mirror, and must push them together and make an art-piece out of it.<br /> Imagine a Changeling game, where even though the Great Shattering has happened, you can still encourage humanity to dream again.<br /> <br /> Or, my personal favorite: A Mummy game where you're going around post-Y2K, being the supernatural therapist to everyone else (and sometimes cracking a few skulls, because not everyone wants to be healed).<br /> <br /> And so-on.<br /> <br /> I'm also just now imagining Big D from Hunter: the Parenting, going around after the Y2K, Anthelios, and Avatar Storm events, helping out stragglers and giving therapy sessions to survivors like they're shiny Pok\u00e9mon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T16:51:03-08:00","id":1556540,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556501#post1556501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pretend the metaplot doesn't exist beyond &quot;an event in the past&quot; and just run your games as you see fit, with the overarching theme being the concept of a Phoenix and rebuilding. That's all I can really offer. Because what the pre-9/11 lore of oWoD offers is hot garbage.</div> </div> </div> </div>The bigger problem with your proposal is that it ignores the game and what makes people like it and throw everything away for a brand-new style that no one asked for.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying this take doesn't have its benefits. This may be an interesting take on the setting, and I would play it myself.<br /> <br /> But... it doesn't really address the question, it just ignores it, refuses to engage.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> nailed it, for WoD5 it's meaningless, for WoD20 it's a dead question, everyone has to settle for either whatever is already there or make their own thing, which is then better left as a personal thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4},{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-13T18:33:10-08:00","id":1556586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556540#post1556540\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> nailed it, for WoD5 it's meaningless, for WoD20 it's a dead question, everyone has to settle for either whatever is already there or make their own thing, which is then better left as a personal thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Touch\u00e9.<br /> <br /> Allow me to explain myself a bit more: For me, the metaplot has always been this bizzare testament to people's anxieties about Y2k and the belief that the world was going to end in Jan 1 of 2000. Which... I honestly have no idea how to react or engage with that.<br /> The closest I could ever get to understanding what that was like, would be 2020 (which itself is a completely different type of situation). But even there: The world itself didn't end - just the global paradigm that Reality itself was built upon (Which to me is much more interesting than &quot;World's screwed because the writers' studio was imploding&quot; And that is ANOTHER can of worms that I don't want to touch, because then my post would just devolve into me rambling for ten pages on how much I loathe the original 1999 release of Hunter: the Reckoning).<br /> <br /> <br /> However, <b>back to topic:</b> What do you do, when a world's end that was foretold in prophecy, arrives... But the world itself doesn't end? All of Creation was supposed to die, and it didn't.<br /> Now what?<br /> <br /> Well, that's the fun part (even if it's not fun for people like me, who just want a straight answer, and end up misinterpreting the information we get because it's more ambiguous by design): The books all straight up say &quot;Above all, have fun.&quot;<br /> <br /> That's where Storytellers can pick up and string together their own head-canons for what did/didn't survive the metaplot. Because from that perspective/angle, the rulebooks are a thick recollection-like description to how things <b>were</b>, not as they <b>are</b>, <b>per storyteller</b> (which, for better or worse, makes each group's games more personalized, with various <i>UNDERSTANDINGS </i>of cannon being explainable as different manifestations of the same setting - as bizarrely meta as that sounds).<br /> <br /> Misunderstandings of the cannon are also a thing that is going to happen (which... guilty as charged), but the books themselves warn the reader &quot;keep it consistent&quot; and to not change decisions based off of how you interpreted a rule/situation at the time (&quot;Vampires don't take extra damage from silver? Crap, uh, okay. I made a mistake this game, but NEXT GAME I'll make sure that I remember that detail, because my players have already injured said vampire with a silver weapon, so I need to stick to my guns and keep it consistent&quot;).<br /> <br /> <br /> The Metaplot exists, and for people who want it in the final stories of their chronicles, that's great. It makes for a cool plot, for the storytellers who can use it for their games.<br /> For people who want to get into the game (let alone RUN the thing), then the metaplot is the last thing on the minds (and that should be a good thing. They should discover and read that material on their own terms instead of having to deal with it right out the gate, because they're already reading several hundred pages of material per rulebook by themselves).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28838,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","user":"DaFrontlineSpecter","date":"Tried to) make my reply more readable. No idea if anything I'm saying makes sense. 12-13-2025, 09:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-14T01:24:45-08:00","id":1556595,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556586#post1556586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For me, the metaplot has always been this bizzare testament to people's anxieties about Y2k and the belief that the world was going to end in Jan 1 of 2000.</div> </div> </div> </div>This seems to be very at odds with what we usually mean when we're talking about &quot;the metaplot.&quot; Metaplots are an ongoing story the occurs within gaming books that leads to significant changes to the default setting (esp. when new editions happen).<br /> <br /> The metaplot didn't put eschatology as a major theme of the WoD. It was always there from the beginning. If the WoD never made it past 1e and thus we never saw any real metaplot develop, the themes of the five original games would still have a major apocalyptic undertone to them because the original creators wanted the tension of impeding cosmic doom to be part of the WoD.<br /> <br /> The idea of it specifically being about Y2K is rather misplaced. The games started - and again, with a theme of the end times being nigh already - before Y2K had become a mainstream public concern; instead of a topic constrained to computer scientists and engineers that were working on the problem and developing the solutions we'd eventually see implemented anyway. The first broad public article on the problem was put out in 1993, and even then the tone was still, &quot;we have seven years to address this, so no need to worry about it, since people have already been working on it for eight years.&quot; The WoD is much more a testament of the stagnation of culture and society in the 80s and 90s; esp. in the US given the creators in question. During a period of domestic prosperity and stability, there was growing counter-culture angst as &quot;trickle down&quot; policy not only was failing to manifest as the powerful preferred negative peace over improving the lives of the poor and minorities; nor addressing big problems abroad as the world got &quot;smaller.&quot; Counter-culture art of various types screamed into a seeming void as the rich got richer and the poor got poorer, despite constant promises that the great economy would lift all boats.<br /> <br /> The metaplot just moved the ingrained eschatology of the games along with the growing discontent at the status quo further along as the editions kept coming until the original Time of Judgement books hit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which... I honestly have no idea how to react or engage with that. </div> </div> </div>While I completely appreciate many reasons why that could be the case, trying to help on this front would require knowing some potentially more personal than you'd like to share things. I was old enough to remember all of this when it was happening, but I've shared a vague sense of my age with this forums for a long time. I'm also an American from a similar background to many of the WoD creators (even if they're older than me and all that), so my cultural reference points easily align to where they drew from.<br /> <br /> Something I will point out though is that eschatology is nothing new. In many ways that's part of the original satire of the WoD: humanity is split between people that are so obsessed with The End that they stop caring about fixing things in case this date for the end is wrong again (just like the last thousand times), and people so put off by taking major catastrophes seriously that getting motivated by the impending climate crisis to organize around doing something about it feels as silly as worrying about having your soul in order for a supernatural doomsday instead. In the WoD in the 90s, the world ending was a literal impending issue, and the only people that really knew what was happening were the last people you would want to listen to, and really not people you should be listening to anyway despite being right about that.<br /> <br /> Human cultures have been through this. A lot. People get heated up about a new prediction for the end and... nothing happens. We have lots and lots of study of what happens to people that bought into the hype after the date turns out to be a dud. Which ranges from acting like it was just a phase and moving on (even if primed to repeat), to obsessively trying to justify how the date was calculated wrong by flawed humans and the new date is totally right for real (to them in their heads this is very true no matter how illogical it might seem to others). <br /> <br /> It's also worth noting that the WoD has always left room for both &quot;linear&quot; and &quot;cyclical&quot; eschatology. Everyone might agree that the End Times were coming, but the books from the start had some people believing in a single end to everything (linear) and others seeing it an and of one world for the birth of another (cyclical). When the original WW pulled the trigger on the ToJ, they included scenarios for both outcomes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> However, <b>back to topic:</b> What do you do, when a world's end that was foretold in prophecy, arrives... But the world itself doesn't end? All of Creation was supposed to die, and it didn't.<br /> Now what? </div> </div> </div>I think I covered the basics above. Now what is going to be different for different people based on the cultural backgrounds, philosophic/theological beliefs, personal investment in a given belief structure that acknowledged said prophecy in the first place and how well it is structured to adapt to such instances, etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's where Storytellers can pick up and string together their own head-canons for what did/didn't survive the metaplot. </div> </div> </div>Sure, that's what playing the 20th editions is basically about at this point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Misunderstandings of the cannon are also a thing that is going to happen (which... guilty as charged), but the books themselves warn the reader &quot;keep it consistent&quot; and to not change decisions based off of how you interpreted a rule/situation at the time (&quot;Vampires don't take extra damage from silver? Crap, uh, okay. I made a mistake this game, but NEXT GAME I'll make sure that I remember that detail, because my players have already injured said vampire with a silver weapon, so I need to stick to my guns and keep it consistent&quot;). </div> </div> </div>While true, I'm not sure what mistakes on interpretation of the canon of the WoD books has to do with what you're talking about.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Metaplot exists,... </div> </div> </div>I think that's a questionable statement for the current state of the game. The ToJ scenarios exist. The metaplot hasn't been supported in over 20 years. Given the &quot;living&quot; nature of a metaplot, when one is no long being actively published, it's hard to consider it to still exist as a metaplot instead of just being the status of the setting when the books stopped doing metaplot (even if the books didn't actually end at that point).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:08:14-08:00","id":1556633,"title":"","content":"Third part is the most important.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556586#post1556586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For me, the metaplot has always been this bizzare testament to people's anxieties about Y2k and the belief that the world was going to end in Jan 1 of 2000. Which... I honestly have no idea how to react or engage with that.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I mostly agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Heavy Arms</a> in this regard, there is something I want to add that makes a big difference:<br /> <br /> The 90's were abnormal in how much eschatology gained terrain in the public's mind. Especially in <i><b>Christian</b></i>.<br /> <br /> As he said, eschatology is an old thing among humans. While it isn't true that it's a feature of every religion as some think, it has been around since before writing. It's a common anxiety among humans the idea of a catastrophe that will endanger the very existence of our people.<br /> <br /> But Christianity specifically is a very Eschatological religion. It <b><u>is</u></b>, fundamentally, about the Time of Judgement. Even if you ignore the Book of Revelations, the foundation of the whole religion is the Second Coming of Jesus and the Judgement that comes with it. You're not expected to follow the rules because this will bring immediate benefits to you or others (even though it may), but because this will decide the fate of your eternal soul in the final battle between the two sides of reality itself.<br /> <br /> For anyone with a Christian upbringing, even if very weak, this is an underlying assumption of existence and everything even tangentially linked to the religion. Regardless if you believe or not it's a constant presence, and if you're a religious person to any degree this is an ever-present thought.<br /> <br /> In the 90's, on top of Christianity being even more widespread than now and people in general even more religious all around the globe, a few things compounded to bring this to a boil: Climate and environment fears were starting to leave the academic niches and really get public attention (hence we had the Earth Summit of 1992). The Cold War just ended, which was in fact an Earth-Shattering event like none since. Apparent political, military and economic stability in the world-stage, give or take local occurrences, gave us a collective ennui that ended up nicknamed &quot;the end of history&quot; by philosophers, an impression that we've reached the pinnacle of social development for better or worse and nothing else of significance would ever happen again. And above all that, playing directly to Christian eschatological anxiety, the Millenium was coming, and that was a very powerful symbol. We do have evidence of a similar uptick in eschatological fears a thousand years before.<br /> <br /> So, what that means? First, while it became famous later as the only real Millenium threat, Y2K wasn't really the cause of such anxieties, it was just one of many random things that got caught up in the very real anxiety and got overblown out of proportion by <i>some</i> people. Second, eschatological fears didn't just became a widespread anxiety through the decade, this greatly affected art and media at the time. Third, it also resulted in the growth of the very real things that people do based on eschatological beliefs: doomsday cults attracted more followers and grew, which by itself leaves a trail of broken families and people, they also got bolder in their actions, that included mass suicides for some and terrorist attacks for others (yes, terrorism gained mainstream attention back in the 90's because of Christian terrorists), here and there individuals were doing those things on their own, too, sometimes taking the lives of their families before their own, or just doing things like sell all their possessions and other life-destroying decisions based on the belief that they were living the last day of their lives.<br /> <br /> Nothing here is an absolute, of course, just a small fraction of everything happening at the time, but the cultural impact was intense. And the World of Darkness was born and further developed against this background. In fact I'm personally of the opinion that a big part of WoD's success was due to how much it spoke to the <i>zeitgeist</i> of the time right out of the bat. Even if note, it's undeniably a satire on it, a response to the social dynamics that both birthed the 90's and that developed through it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556586#post1556586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do you do, when a world's end that was foretold in prophecy, arrives... But the world itself doesn't end? All of Creation was supposed to die, and it didn't.<br /> Now what?</div> </div> </div> </div>Everything goes on as normal, including the fears and prophecies of the end. It's not as common nowadays, but widespread apocalyptic prophecies popped out sometimes several times a year during the 90's and their failures never changed anything. And they didn't change now, they just became quieter as the mainstream attention wandered elsewhere. Doomsday cults still exist, there are still people talking about the end or throwing away their lives (figuratively or literally) because of it, the end is still a popular artistic theme, Christianity is as eschatological as ever and growing in power again (it's in fact more politically powerful now than in the 90's), people still fear a lot of things out of proportion in a manner that is in many ways an apocalyptic belief (social media, AI, climate change, industrialization, space race, cold war, economic collapse, dictatorships, religious war, national isolation, pandemics, mental health problems, anti-tech movements, communism, environmentalism, de-industrialization, space threats, new alliances, anarcho-capitalism, weak governments, the woke, globalization, vaccines, mental health worries, Nazism...), with the compounding problem that many of those are legitimate fears that do have a chance at ending society even at reasonable proportions. Conspiracy theories are in many ways a new form of doomsday cult, by the way.<br /> <br /> Literally none of those ceased to exist, they just changed a little and became less obvious, or get less mainstream attention.<br /> <br /> Despite how the metaplot ended, as a core theme eschatology in the game has never been about a factual risk of it happening (although this risk being real helps set the mood), but about the things people do because of it. And people do a lot of things because of it. Just look at how the sects are shaped by it, the Sabbat preparing for the final battle against an existential threat of world-ending proportions, the Camarilla being defined not by indifference to the prophecies, but a policy of active denial, at the same time that a lot of it's actual culture is an ever-present fear and acts of destruction against prophetic signs in the hopes that this will keep the cataclysm at bay.<br /> <br /> Nothing here really requires for the prophecy to be real. And just as in human society, there's no real date to it, nothing really says that it happens shortly after 2004. It has been expected for thousands of years to start at any time now, great upheavals happened dozens of times that were taken as the end finally coming and then passed, this is just one more weird period in the long history of the Kindred where the world seemed to be on the brink, it happened many times before, it will happen many times again.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556586#post1556586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For people who want to get into the game (let alone RUN the thing), then the metaplot is the last thing on the minds (and that should be a good thing. They should discover and read that material on their own terms instead of having to deal with it right out the gate, because they're already reading several hundred pages of material per rulebook by themselves).</div> </div> </div> </div>No one is required to learn the metaplot to run or play the game and I'm unaware of anyone here suggesting new player to try. It's generally a bad idea.<br /> <br /> But you're not just maybe overwhelmed with it, the most important thing is that you're seeing the metaplot as cause, when it is effect.<br /> <br /> The game has a proposal. It has literary premises, genres, themes and mood. It is about the kind of stories it tries to facilitate. And those are the things that attract most people to it.<br /> <br /> Of course no one is required to follow through, and there are many potential benefits on breaking the mold. But when we talk about offering a solution to the community, the community expects something that stay true to those, not necessarily to the metaplot, because those are the community's unifying interests.<br /> <br /> Your proposal isn't a good fit for this thread's underlying proposal (discussing a solution to perceived metaplot problems) because it does the exact opposite: it retains the metaplot intact, but throw away almost everything that defines the game for something else completely different. This isn't something many fans will receive well in this sense, even those that still like the proposal itself.<br /> <br /> And to be clear: there's nothing wrong <i>in general</i> with your idea, it just doesn't fit this purpose. If you want to run it, you're welcome to make a thread about it, to discuss and build. You won't have any assurance that people will want to engage (and I think every regular poster has their share of ill received or ignored threads), but there's nothing wrong with trying.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:58:28-08:00","id":1556636,"title":"","content":"I'd quibble back a bit on one point:<br /> <br /> The eschatology fervor was abnormally high in the global zeitgeist of the 90s. For US Christianity... it was fairly normal if a bit louder.<br /> <br /> Ever since the Second Great Awakening in the 1820s, the Protestant majority of the US also turned heavily into an Evangelical plurality and with that came a strong adoption of rapture-theology as doctrine or dogma among the different mainly US-specific Christian movements. As the Catholic Churches, the Orthodox Churches, the Church of England and thus most Anglicans, the traditional European Lutherans and most Lutherans in the rest of the world, all reject rapture eschatology as doctrine or dogma (though there's diversity in tolerance of belief in it as tradition or preference), most of the Christian world doesn't have the same level of base-line eschatology-obsession that the US has. It correlates highly to how abnormally religious the US is based on factors that predict religiosity from the psychological study of religion.<br /> <br /> When you consider that for the last 150 years or so, 30-40% of the people in the US were raised on the specific eschatology of rapture theology as doctrine, it shouldn't be surprising that we have a significant ebb and flow of eschatology hype that tends to mirror the political climate of the era. Pretty much every sense of major social change or upheaval was seen by at least someone as a sign of the Tribulation starting. Civil War? Jim Crow entrenchment? Great Depression? WWI? WWII? The Civil Rights Movement? The push back against the successful expansion of civil rights? The quasi-Awakening movement now called the Silent Majority (which is still active in US even if that name is more associated with a few decades ago)? All of those came with major eschatology movements as well.<br /> <br /> I have no studies I can point to on this, but in my experience non-US Christians that know what Christian Millenialism is tend to know it isn't actually linked to the years that are multiples of 1000, while US Christians tend to be more drawn in by the symmetry of that (though they don't all append it to the calendar... Young Earth Creationist sects sometimes try to base it on 6000 years since Creation, with 7000 starting to creep in a bit as time moves forward) without realizing they're unrelated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-15T06:13:03-08:00","id":1556642,"title":"","content":"Now that you mention, I really noticed how much Rapture is common on US arts and religious discourse. Around here it's far from a standard, although I'm under the impression that it's becoming more common.<br /> <br /> It's important though to observe that there's an important distinction between knowing your theology and having an emotional response to something, especially when it's a common fear.<br /> <br /> People instinctively react to those symmetries. Probably a byproduct of our constant search for patterns and meaning, the idea of the turn of the millennium <i>felt</i> powerful regardless of actual beliefs all by itself. While that alone doesn't mean much, for a people that do have a concept of what the most important event ever will be and tacitly expect it to happen any day &quot;without warning&quot; (even if not really most of the time), the association between the two becomes inevitable, if just as a &quot;hunch&quot;.<br /> <br /> Most Christians I knew at the time (which were almost everyone I knew, period) had at least this unease. In fact, even agnostics frequently had it. It didn't always correlate to any real belief that it would happen, most people didn't, but the tension was there.<br /> <br /> And since even in a highly centralized church like the Roman Apostolic Catholic most people actually follow personal and local traditions and folklore more than understand the official doctrine and dogma... yeah, a lot of people that wouldn't know Millennialism as a formal concept still put in their heads that it made sense, to varying degrees. Again, most didn't really <i>believe</i> it, but there was a lot of uncertainty and some . All adding up to the fact that an increase implies a baseline presence, I've heard myself a few weeks ago of an old man in my area who find and buy into a new Rapture prophecy almost every year, tells the whole family, prepares himself to be taken, then stays grumpy a whole week because it failed, sometimes in front of everyone.<br /> <br /> Back in 1996 we had here in Brazil even a telenovela called <i>O Fim do Mundo</i> (The End of The World) that had a lot of the vibes of WoD's eschatology, but more as a comedy. A Psychic that's revered as a miracle worker by some and seen as a phony by others suddenly prophecise the end will come in just 3 months. From then on, as people react to this in a variety of ways, all kinds of weird phenomena start to happen in the city in a slow crescendo of the bizarre. The mood in the city is of increasing fear, then panic, while it explores how different people react to it, from those who believe right away to those who stay skeptic to the end.<br /> <br /> Which isn't an end as nothing happens, nature goes back to normal and everyone has to face the consequences of the things they did during the upheaval. It's not Brazillian TV at its best, but is one of the few telenovelas I watched, and is short with just 35 episodes. In the original run, though, it had a written epilogue where the fates of the characters for the following years of normalcy is given, then at the very moment 1999 ends the Earth explodes. This was removed in a reprise that run in 2000.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-21T16:53:22-08:00","id":1556884,"title":"","content":"While I agree with most here that it doesn't matter since the metaplot is capital D dead. I agree some gamelines would've had utterly different metaplot, but not for the reasons you implied. It's not about theme, but rather the stability of the setting. <br /> <br /> when you look at most wod gamelines, they are &quot;stable&quot; meaning you can extrapolate them from publishing era to today without much an issue. <br /> <br /> For exemple, if people in 2070 were to reopen a vtm book, they'd have no issue imagining the Camarilla, sabbat and co lurking in the shadows of the 70s. <br /> <br /> That's because while these gamelines reacted to real world events, they were not so much about breaking real life continuity of history. <br /> <br /> Except for two of them, werewolf and mage. <br /> <br /> Werewolf assumes a pr\u00e9 apocalyptic/beginning of the apocalypse standpoint. From the very first corebook we have packs of European werewolves doing ecoterrorism to the point the book states the veil might be lifted in Europe soon, red talons attacking human settlements, and in general a mood of &quot;everything is going to explode&quot;. <br /> <br /> So it wouldn't make sense for wta to follow our real life continuity. There's no way life is going on as it did for us when planetary aspects are corrupted (see under a blood red moon), shadow lords attack nuclear sites in Europe, fomori rise in numbers and so on. In 2025, the world should have entered the apocalypse. <br /> <br /> Same for Mage. The whole point of pages was powerful factions fighting to shape the world. In first and second edition, the traditions were achieving much across the world, the ascension war ran hot. 2025 should be as different from the 90s as the 90s from Victorian age. <br /> <br /> <br /> So the fact that wta and mtas are part of the wod metaplot may have gone to their detriment. While the normal state of the wod is that &quot;the world is as you know it, but with dark conspiracies behind it&quot; is beneficial to every other game line, it makes both of these two essentially inepts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-21T17:39:44-08:00","id":1556885,"title":"","content":"I agree. Werewolf in particular assumes a metaplot where the Apocalypse is rapidly approaching.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-21T19:24:05-08:00","id":1556889,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556885#post1556885\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree. Werewolf in particular assumes a metaplot where the Apocalypse is rapidly approaching.</div> </div> </div> </div>Vampires assumed Gehenna was near, Wraiths and Changelings had their own ideas of crisis. <br /> <br /> For me the key point is simply that Vampires and Wraiths are stuck in cycles of decay, Werewolves are fighting a force of decay gone mad, and Mages and Changelings have options. Vampires, Wraiths and Garou are tragic figures by design and destiny. Mages and Changelings are also the heroes of their stories but they get a say, not easy to use, as to whether their stories are tragic, comic, dramatic or romantic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-21T20:51:23-08:00","id":1556892,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556889#post1556889\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Vampires assumed Gehenna was near, Wraiths and Changelings had their own ideas of crisis.<br /> <br /> For me the key point is simply that Vampires and Wraiths are stuck in cycles of decay, Werewolves are fighting a force of decay gone mad, and Mages and Changelings have options. Vampires, Wraiths and Garou are tragic figures by design and destiny. Mages and Changelings are also the heroes of their stories but they get a say, not easy to use, as to whether their stories are tragic, comic, dramatic or romantic.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> The thing is, gehenna is coming. Soon...... Soon.... <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b. Okay now it's cyclical but soon<br /> <br /> Okay, sarcasm off, but the point is here. Gehenna is always at the horizon, it was at the horizon during the cainite heresy, when the inquisition rose, when the Giovanni destroyed the cappdocians. Gehenna will come when the antediluvians/caine/god/etc will decide it does. <br /> <br /> So it my well be that life and un life goes on for the next two centuries before the old prophecies finally kick off. In the meantime, human history, at least in appearance, is the same as ours while sects and clans manipulates and fight int he shadows. <br /> <br /> Same for wraith and changeling. There may well be centuries of ebb and flows of glamour and banality, new resurgence like event, and new approach of winter, while the world looks the same to those that don't see the dreaming. And new great maelstrom may appear, maybe even wrecking stygia and the other kingdoms of the dead, with invisible impact on the living. <br /> <br /> For all of them, gehenna, the final winter or the ultimate return of spring, the last maelstrom or the tearing of the shroud, any events that would shatter the illusion of the normal world may happen tomorrow or next millenia. <br /> <br /> <br /> But that's not the case for werewolf and mages. Garou are already acting in more nd more reckless ways toward humanity, there's no way the status quo last even a few decades more with the state of wta's metzplot even in 1st Ed. <br /> <br /> Same for mages, neither traditions nor technocracy seeks the status quo. The traditions have found unity in the 2nd edition and there's not stopping until the technocratic paradigm is wrecked. And the techno have their own plans to implement radical tech in the consensus. <br /> <br /> And that's what I'm going at. Most of the game lines can follow the &quot;our world, but monsters are hidden within&quot; theme. But mges and werewolf are wrecking balls against that very theme.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-23T23:58:58-08:00","id":1556953,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyremne</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556892#post1556892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And that's what I'm going at. Most of the game lines can follow the &quot;our world, but monsters are hidden within&quot; theme. But mges and werewolf are wrecking balls against that very theme.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that misses a few things.<br /> <br /> First, one thing I didn't said before because it seemed kind of pointless, but the reality is that the Metaplot was always a Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat, because the WoD is a Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat. There's no WoD. There has never been a single setting where everything is happening. Each gameline was written primarily as a standalone setting and crossovers were an inconsistent mess about how much any is canon to the other.<br /> <br /> So there is no stability of the setting, as there never existed a stable unified setting to begin with. Their very premises and cosmology are frequently at odds with the others' premises and cosmology. I can't picture a world where vampires rule cities from the dark, yet have no idea about Umbrae and whatnot that's regular fare for the others, or one where powerful Magical conspiracies stir the winds of history but can't notice the obvious signs of a corrupting super-demon of entropy who commands legions of mutants eating away at society.<br /> <br /> They are fundamentally incompatible, period. Any crossover either follows something that isn't the actual setting of neither gameline involved, or follows one and the other is haphazardly thrown in. Usually either is done through glossing over inconsistencies and pretending they're not there, or not really thinking their necessary ramifications.<br /> <br /> But on Mage, specifically, the game isn't about this war really coming to an end even if we ignore <i>Revised</i> (that stopped the war on its tracks). In Mage this is how history have always been. Mages stir society all the time and are equally affected by it, and they clash with each other too much for any to gain a definitive upper hand. Society isn't a passive victim, either, even if individual Sleepers can't do much, a lot of things do not happen because Mages want, they just are. A lot os in the air about how much the Technocracy is really responsible for current technology, or how much the Consensus <i>Theory</i> is right, or even if it is right how much Mages can really affect it.<br /> <br /> Nor do most Mages on any of the two groups want to completely overhaul reality, and even those who want know they can't. For most Mages it's about people becoming more receptive to their own Paradigm, and not the world becoming a Magical Utopia. Actually even less than that, for most Mages it's about having the support network to do their stuff more easily and protection against interlopers, and they don't really care all that much on a personal sense about how the world will be like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-01T23:50:19-08:00","id":1557202,"title":"","content":"I'll agree with Astromancer on one point: <b>Mage</b> is different from nearly every other World of Darkness gameline in that it never really had a doom scenario of its own. Just look at the titles of the final books (or chapters in <b>Time of Judgment</b>, for those gamelines that ended there) and you can see a difference: <b>Vampire</b> got <b>Gehenna</b>; <b>Werewolf</b> got <b>Apocalypse</b>; <b>Wraith</b>, which had died five years earlier in <b>Ends of Empire</b>, got a last hurrah of sorts in <b>Orpheus</b>, which ended with <b>End Game</b>. <b>Changeling</b> ended with <i>Eternal Winter</i>; <b>Demon</b> ended with <i>Agents of the Apocalypse</i>; <b>Kindred of the East</b> ended with <i>Interesting Times</i> (a Chinese curse); and <b>Mummy</b> ended with <i>Armageddon</i>. <br /> <br /> <b>Mage</b>? It ended with <b>Ascension</b>. The only other gameline that didn't end on a downer title was <b>Hunter</b>, which got to <i>Inherit the Earth </i>(that is, it ended with the monsters being vanquished, and humanity picking up the pieces and rebuilding in the post-Darkness world). Note that <b>Mage</b> <i>could</i> have ended with <b>Descension</b> as its title, had the authors wanted to emphasize a more nihilistic view of the end of the line; and that would have been in keeping with how nearly all other gamelines ended. But they didn't. Instead, they chose to end on a high note. <br /> <br /> And to emphasize further how different <b>Mage</b> is from the other gamelines: the concept of Ascension in <b>Mage</b> isn't inherently an &quot;end times&quot; thing. <i>Mass</i> Ascension can be argued to be an &quot;end times&quot; thing; but by default, &quot;Ascension&quot; in <b>Mage</b> refers to something more personal: the triumphant end of one mage's story. In my experience, <b>Mage</b>'s metaplot never had the same weight that the metaplot had for the other gamelines: even before the Revised Edition split the fan base with the rise of the Avatar Storm, most <b>Mage</b> fans were more inclined to complain about how those metaplot elements that existed were getting in the way of the stories that <i>they</i> wanted to tell. Rather than conveying a sense of a living, growing world, <b>Mage</b>'s metaplot was viewed by most as an inconvenience. <br /> <br /> To me, it's not even a matter of <b>Mage</b>'s metaplot following a different trajectory than the other gamelines; it's that its metaplot simply didn't carry the same <i>relevance</i> as the other gamelines' metaplots carried.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-02T00:02:15-08:00","id":1557203,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1556953#post1556953\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think that misses a few things.<br /> <br /> First, one thing I didn't said before because it seemed kind of pointless, but the reality is that the Metaplot was always a Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat, because the WoD is a Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat. There's no WoD. There has never been a single setting where everything is happening. Each gameline was written primarily as a standalone setting and crossovers were an inconsistent mess about how much any is canon to the other.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, <b>Ascension</b> all but says as much, with its &quot;Fractured Cosmos&quot; section. It suggests that if you want to, you can view the so-called &quot;world&quot; of Darkness as a series of parallel worlds coupled to each other through resonant events. According to the Fractured Cosmos model, the various parallels don't even operate on the same metaphysics, reflecting how you really do need to pick one gameline as the primary source for your chronicle, with all others playing subordinate roles. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-06T02:00:02-08:00","id":1557335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1557202#post1557202\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To me, it's not even a matter of <b>Mage</b>'s metaplot following a different trajectory than the other gamelines; it's that its metaplot simply didn't carry the same <i>relevance</i> as the other gamelines' metaplots carried.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's so much so that even in a game where you take the metaplot at face value, it never meant anything like the end of the world (even if just the end of &quot;our splat's world&quot;) was about to happen. The Avatar Storm and every other sign of doom is just a precursor to <i>something</i> happening. The events may change the world forever, but that isn't new and there will still be a world after that to keep living in, doing stuff and changing it.<br /> <br /> The only scenario in which we can say Mage has an End of the World prediction is the <b><i>complete, final</i></b> victory of the <b>Nefandi</b>, but this has never been hinted as something to worry that much unless you want it for a very specific chronicle.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547856-maybe-we-should-split-the-metaplot?p=1557203#post1557203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">According to the Fractured Cosmos model, the various parallels don't even operate on the same metaphysics, reflecting how you really do need to pick one gameline as the primary source for your chronicle, with all others playing subordinate roles.</div> </div> </div> </div>And I think this is something people should do more, as it creates more interesting chronicles <i><b>precisely</b></i> because crossover assuming a primary gameline forces you to bend the others and come up with more creative reasons for other splats to work while &quot;subordinate&quot; to the main line of the chronicle.<br /> <br /> How do the many secret societies and manipulators interact? How do they operate with the others around? Most crossover games have a fuzzy hierarchy of Conspiracies, but in practice those conspiracies seldom if ever interact. Camarilla, Technocracy and Pentex simply go past each other in a daily basis as if their plans didn't affect the others. How about changing that, and bringing the others under the very real notice of one? That's something you can do shamelessly in a game where you already assume one splat not only as the focus, but as &quot;the setting&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-09T06:42:44-07:00","id":1558749,"title":"","content":"I'm also thinking of this &quot;one of these things isn't like the others&quot; observation and applying it to the post-WoD/CoD Onyx Path now finds itself in, with Curseborne replacing WoD/CoD in terms of the way Vampire, Werewolf, and Wraith were designed, and Scion replacing WoD/CoD in terms of the way Mage and perhaps Changeling were designed. For instance, I get much more of a Mage vibe from Scion's Sorcerers than from Curseborne's Sorcerers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}